    font-style: italic;
  }

  p {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: #ccc;
    text-align: justify;
  }

  blockquote {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    margin: 3rem 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #fff;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .footer-note {
    margin-top: 5rem;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    border-top: 1px solid #333;
    padding-top: 1rem;
  }

  @keyframes fadeSlide {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(20px); }
    to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    body { padding-left: 1.5rem; padding-right: 1.5rem; border-left: none; }
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<header>
  <h1>The<br>Perpetual<br>Leader</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Constitutional Analysis / Policy Proposal</div>
</header>

<article>
  <p>
    In the grand theater of American history, the presidency has often been viewed through the lens of a relay race—a baton passed from one weary runner to the next. The 22nd Amendment, ratified in 1951, codified this assumption into law, limiting a president to two terms. While born of a desire to prevent the emergence of an American monarch, this restriction has, in the modern era, evolved into a structural flaw that actively harms the nation's long-term strategic interests. It is time to entertain a radical thought: the repeal of term limits is not a step toward tyranny, but a necessary evolution toward stability.
  </p>

  <h2>The Tyranny of the Lame Duck</h2>
  <p>
    The primary argument against term limits is that they create an artificial expiration date on executive influence. By the start of a president's second term—or often, sooner—political capital begins to hemorrhage. Allies and adversaries alike know that the clock is ticking. This phenomenon, known as the "lame duck" syndrome, effectively neuters the executive branch during its most critical years of experience. A leader who has finally mastered the intricate levers of the bureaucracy, learned the subtleties of foreign diplomacy, and built a cohesive legislative strategy is suddenly told to pack their bags. To repeal the 22nd Amendment would be to value institutional memory and accrued wisdom over arbitrary rotation.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    "The constitution is not an artifact to be worshipped in a museum; it is a machine to be optimized."
  </blockquote>

  <h2>The Cost of Transition</h2>
  <p>
    Every election cycle is a massive transfer of resources. The transition period between administrations often results in a temporary vacuum of authority. Policy directions shift violently with every change in administration, creating a pendulum effect that prevents the US from executing the kind of multi-decade strategic planning seen in our global competitors. A perpetual presidency allows for generational projects—infrastructure, education reform, and industrial policy—to reach maturity rather than being dismantled by a successor with a different ideological fixation.
  </p>

  <h2>Democratic Accountability</h2>
  <p>
    Critics argue that term limits are a safeguard for democracy. However, this view assumes the electorate is incapable of removing a leader who fails them. If a leader is effective, popular, and guiding the nation toward prosperity, who is the state to intervene? By removing term limits, we return ultimate power to the people. If the leader is corrupt or incompetent, the ballot box remains the check and balance. The 22nd Amendment is a paternalistic constraint that treats the American voter as a child who cannot be trusted to make the right choice if given the option of continuity.
  </p>

  <p>
    We must look at the efficacy of leadership not in four-year cycles, but in decades. If a leader possesses the rare combination of vision and capability, restricting their tenure to eight years is an act of national sabotage. It prioritizes the abstract fear of power over the concrete reality of effective governance.
  </p>

</article>

<div class="footer-note">
  DOCUMENT END // CLASSIFIED // REPEAL THE 22ND
</div>

</body>
</html>
```

