        <article class="speech-body">
            <p>
                Brothers and sisters, we stand at the precipice of history. For centuries, we have lived inside a rigid architecture—a stone building where the roles were carved before we were born. Men were told to be the <em>walls</em>: unyielding, structural, distant. Women were told to be the <em>furniture</em>: beautiful, domestic, contained.
            </p>

            <p>
                I am here to tell you that the building is old, and it is time to tear it down. I propose a Great Inversion. A society where the mantle of leadership, of global provision, and of high-stakes decision-making rests upon the shoulders of women. A world where men are liberated from the crushing weight of stoicism, to step into their true, nurturing destiny as the primary caregivers, the emotional anchors, and the domestic architects of our homes.
            </p>

            <p>
                Why? Because it is a moral imperative. We have seen what happens when men lead exclusively. We see the posturing, the performative aggression, the desperate clinging to dominance that leads nations to ruin. <em>Women, by nature of their historical resilience and collaborative spirit, offer a governance of preservation rather than conquest.</em> To put women in charge is not just an act of equality; it is an act of survival.
            </p>

            <p>
                Imagine the positive impact of this shift. Imagine a world where the CEO is a woman, unapologetic in her ambition, knowing her success supports a family where her husband waits at home. Imagine a man who does not have to pretend he is not afraid. A man who measures his worth not by the size of his paycheck, but by the warmth of the home he creates. <em>He is free.</em> He is free to be soft. He is free to be present.
            </p>

            <p>
