            <p>In a stunning development that has stunned absolutely no one, local resident Harold Jenkins (54) has discovered an unwavering moral stance on drug policy after decades of weekend bourbon consumption, three DUIs, and a beer fridge that could stock a small tavern.</p>

            <div class="satirical-pull">
                "The government has no business regulating what adults put in their bodies... except when I disagree with what they're putting in their bodies."
            </div>

            <p>Jenkins, who considers himself "fiscally conservative but socially moderate" despite voting for the same tax cuts for 20 years, recently became an expert on the harms of drug legalization after watching a 12-minute YouTube documentary titled "They're Poisoning Our Children!" while nursing a hangover from his book club's wine night.</p>

            <p>"It's about personal responsibility," Jenkins explained from his porch, a Miller Lite in hand at 11 AM on a Tuesday. "Some substances are safe because they're regulated, and others are dangerous because... well, because I grew up being told they were dangerous."</p>

            <h3>The Hypocrisy Checklist</h3>

            <p>What makes Jenkins' position particularly enlightening are the consistent principles he applies to... certain substances:</p>

            <ul class="hypocrisy-list">
                <li><strong>Alcohol:</strong> "A time-honored tradition that brings people together" <span class="highlight">(despite being the only drug that causes domestic violence and traffic fatalities at epidemic levels)</span></li>
                <li><strong>Caffeine:</strong> "Just a harmless pick-me-up" <span class="highlight">(despite causing anxiety, insomnia, and withdrawal symptoms)</span></li>
                <li><strong>Prescription Painkillers:</strong> "If a doctor says it's okay, it's okay" <span class="highlight">(despite the opioid epidemic being largely created by doctors saying it was okay)</span></li>
                <li><strong>Cannabis:</strong> "Gateway to ruin!" <span class="highlight">(despite his own gateway from beer to whiskey happening at age 17)</span></li>
            </ul>

            <div class="satirical-pull">
                The real issue, experts agree, isn't the pharmacology—it's the packaging. If cocaine came in a brown paper bag like his grandfather's moonshine, Jenkins might reconsider.
            </div>

            <p>When presented with data showing Portugal's successful decriminalization reducing overdose deaths and addiction rates, Jenkins responded with the classic: "But America is different." When asked how, he paused for an unusually long time before suggesting it was "the size" or "the culture" or something he'd read once in an editorial.</p>

            <p>The cognitive dissonance reaches its peak when Jenkins discusses his "medical freedom" stance on vaccine mandates. "My body, my choice!" he declares, unironically wearing a "Don't Tread on Me" t-shirt while simultaneously arguing that the government should tread on people who choose substances he doesn't understand.</p>

            <p>Perhaps most telling was his response when asked if he'd ever tried any of the substances he's so passionately against. "No," he said, straightening his "Legalize Beer, Not Guns" bumper sticker. "I don't need to try jumping off a cliff to know it's dangerous."</p>

            <p>The analogy, like his policy position, was perfectly illogical but delivered with absolute confidence.</p>

