        </div>

        <p>The fact that maintaining thousands of nuclear weapons creates the very threat their proponents claim to defend against apparently never crosses this line of reasoning. It's the perfect closed loop of fear: We need nukes because others have nukes, and others have nukes because we have nukes. The solution? More nukes, obviously.</p>

        <h3>ARGUMENT #2: "NUCLEAR DETERRENCE HAS KEPT THE PEACE FOR 75 YEARS"</h3>

        <p>Proponents point to the lack of direct conflict between nuclear powers as evidence that the system works. This argument conveniently ignores several minor historical footnotes: the Cuban Missile Crisis, multiple near-launches due to false alarms, the ongoing nuclear arms race, and the existential dread that forms the foundation of modern international relations. It's like crediting a loaded gun on a dinner table for preventing fights at a family gathering—while ignoring the palpable tension and the occasional time someone accidentally knocks it over.</p>

        <p>The fact that humanity has survived despite possessing the means to end itself is less a testament to the wisdom of deterrence and more evidence of our collective, stubborn refusal to actually use the apocalyptic tools we've built. We're essentially applauding ourselves for not shooting ourselves in the foot while standing in a minefield we constructed.</p>

