        <p>Perhaps the most honest argument is also the most absurd: that nuclear weapons serve as status symbols in the international community. In this view, giving up nukes would be like a country voluntarily resigning from the UN Security Council or a CEO giving up their corner office. The prestige of being able to end civilization in 30 minutes apparently outweighs the practical concerns of actually ending civilization.</p>

        <p>The irony here is thick enough to stop a bullet—though not, it should be noted, a nuclear warhead. We maintain the ability to destroy entire cities multiple times over because other countries might think less of us if we didn't. It's the geopolitical equivalent of keeping a flamethrower in your garage because the neighborhood watch might not take you seriously otherwise.</p>

        <h3>THE REAL "RADICAL" IDEA</h3>

        <p>The truly radical position, it turns out, isn't proposing unilateral disarmament. It's suggesting that a world with thousands of nuclear weapons spread across multiple countries, with constant hair-trigger alerts and near-misses, might not be the epitome of security. That the resources spent on maintaining these arsenals could address actual threats like climate change, pandemics, or the erosion of democratic institutions.</p>

